# Line-ending policy: store ALL text files as LF in the repository.
#
# `text=auto` lets Git detect text vs binary and normalize text to LF on commit
# (a Windows working tree still checks out CRLF when core.autocrlf=true, but the
# repo always holds LF). Putting it here makes the policy repo-enforced and
# portable, rather than depending on each contributor's `core.autocrlf` setting.
* text=auto

# ─── Explicit text types (stored as LF) ──────────────────────────────────────
*.rs     text
*.toml   text
*.lock   text
*.md     text
*.json   text
*.py     text
*.ld     text
*.conf   text

# ─── Scripts that MUST stay LF even on a Windows checkout ─────────────────────
*.sh     text eol=lf

# gsh scripts are BAKED INTO THE SHELL BINARY and their SIZE is asserted at compile time:
#
#     const _: () = assert!(SELFCHECK_GS.len() < 65536, "selfcheck.gsh exceeds the ... ceiling");
#
# so their byte count must not depend on which machine checked the repository out. `*.gsh text`
# alone normalizes to LF in the REPO and converts to native on checkout - CRLF on Windows - which
# adds one byte per line. `selfcheck.gsh` is 65139 bytes as stored and 66275 with CRLF, so the
# Windows checkout crossed a ceiling the Linux one cleared, and the v0.19.0 release build failed
# on a machine where nothing was wrong with the source.
#
# The ceiling is not decoration: `prescan_fns` uses u16 offsets, so a script past 64 KiB makes the
# interpreter dispatch the WRONG FUNCTION BODY silently. The assert exists because the failure it
# replaces is invisible (`backlog/40`).
*.gsh    text eol=lf
*.gs     text eol=lf

# Conformance UI fixtures, for the SAME reason as the two rules above: a `.case` file is a
# FIXTURE whose bytes are asserted, not source this machine reads. `scripts/conform.py` parses
# one with `newline=""` deliberately - `crlf-in-a-boot-config.case` PLANTS a CRLF file, and
# universal newlines destroyed that plant once - so the `--- plant ---` / `--- with ---`
# separators are compared exactly. Under `* text=auto` alone a Windows checkout turns them into
# CRLF, `--- with ---` stops matching, and both `mode: replace` cases fail with a confusing
# error on a machine where nothing is wrong with the source. The repo held LF throughout, so
# Linux CI saw nothing: `--selftest` reported 24 of 24 there and 21 of 24 here.
*.case   text eol=lf

# ─── BOOT CONFIGS, read by a BOOTLOADER rather than by this machine ───────────
#
# Same class as `*.sh` above and for a sharper reason: nothing here is ever read by
# Windows. `*.conf text` alone means "normalize to LF on commit, convert to NATIVE on
# checkout" - and native on a Windows checkout is CRLF. The repo held LF, the working
# tree held CRLF, and `Copy-Item` put CRLF on the card.
#
# U-Boot's extlinux parser then treated the trailing `\r` as part of the FILENAME and
# every entry failed with `Failed to load '/godspeed-riscv64-visionfive.img'` - while
# the MENU still rendered perfectly, because a trailing `\r` in a display string just
# returns the cursor and is invisible. That cost two card reflashes and two wrong
# theories (`backlog/26`).
#
# The boot directory is the whole rule rather than a list of extensions, because the
# next board will bring a file type nobody thought to add.
boot/**   text eol=lf

# ...EXCEPT the binaries in it. `boot/**` above marks everything text, which would have git
# "normalize" a device tree blob - 58 KB of binary silently rewritten. Caught by
# `scripts/line_ending_check.py` on its first run, before any checkout acted on it. A later rule
# wins in this file, so this must stay BELOW the line above.
*.dtb    binary

# ─── Binary: never normalize line endings, never diff as text ─────────────────
*.img    binary
*.bin    binary
*.elf    binary
*.efi    binary
*.iso    binary
*.o      binary
*.a      binary
*.png    binary
*.bmp    binary
*.ppm    binary
*.zip    binary
*.gz     binary

# ─── Vendor firmware: the bytes are the vendor's, never git's to rewrite ──────
# Every file under nonfree/ is checked against the SHA-256 its PROVENANCE records, and some are TEXT
# (the CYW43455's NVRAM, `brcmfmac43455-sdio.txt`). `* text=auto` checked that one out with CRLF on
# Windows, so the digest no longer matched and the v0.22.0 release build failed at `nonfree_check` -
# and a Pi 4 image built from such a checkout embedded NVRAM that was not the vendor's. Binary means
# the working copy is the stored bytes on every OS. A later rule wins, so this stays at the end.
nonfree/** binary
